(() => {
  'use strict';
  const table = (head, rows) => `<div class="table-wrap"><table><thead><tr>${head.map(x => `<th scope="col">${x}</th>`).join('')}</tr></thead><tbody>${rows.map(row => `<tr>${row.map(x => `<td>${x}</td>`).join('')}</tr>`).join('')}</tbody></table></div>`;
  const note = (title, body) => `<aside class="note"><strong>${title}</strong><p>${body}</p></aside>`;
  const topic = (id, title, text) => `<a class="topic-link" href="#${id}"><strong>${title}</strong><p>${text}</p><span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>`;
  const element = (name, color, role, numbers, detail) => `<section class="element" style="--accent:${color}"><header><h3>${name}</h3><span class="tag">${role}</span></header><div class="numbers">${numbers.map(([value, label]) => `<div><strong>${value}</strong><span>${label}</span></div>`).join('')}</div>${detail}</section>`;
  window.INKY_GUIDE = [
    {
      id: 'start', nav: 'Start here', eyebrow: 'WELCOME TO THE OVERHAUL', title: 'Survive the rush.<br>Build your power.',
      intro: 'Inky Zombies builds a run around movement, headshots and personal progression. Learn to handle a demanding opening, then grow into stronger weapons, expanded perks, elemental attacks and airborne Exo movement while the hordes push back.',
      keywords: 'install subscribe load mod beginner first game first five rounds survival tips learning curve classic training sandbox version 1.3 loaded dice boss rush new update zombie difficulty heroic nightmare scaling capped pack pap price',
      body: `<div class="actions"><a class="action" href="#controls">Learn the controls →</a><a class="action secondary" href="#overcharge">Explore the elements</a></div>
      <div class="stat-strip"><div class="stat"><strong>130</strong><span>starting maximum HP</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>3</strong><span>primary weapon slots</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>18</strong><span>perks in the full roster</span></div><div class="stat"><strong>4</strong><span>Overcharge elements</span></div></div>
      ${note('Inky Zombies 1.3 - Loaded Dice Update', 'Choose <b>Loaded Dice</b> for <b>3× / 4× / 5× roll weight</b> on eligible Wonder Weapons and special box tacticals across rounds <b>1–9 / 10–19 / 20+</b>. Ordinary spins cost <b>2,000 points</b>. Usury payoff and Boss Rush grant the bonus without its tax; Boss Rush earns Loaded Dice on <b>round 27</b> and scar-free Overcharge on <b>round 33</b>. See <a href="#blessings">Spiritual Blessings</a> and the <a href="#boss-rush">Boss Rush guide</a>.')}
      ${note('Map and co-op conveniences', 'Der Eisendrache has easier Void sacrifices, credit for zombie kills while dragons feed, a flexible plunger clock and more forgiving wisp timing. Zetsubou starts you with a golden bucket. Teammates can roll their own copies of unlocked box weapons, and buyers can melee to share a ready Wunderfizz bottle. See <a href="#maps">Maps &amp; compatibility</a> and <a href="#economy">Co-op purchases</a>.')}
      ${note('Customizable difficulty and pricing', 'Hosts can tune <b>Zombie Difficulty</b> (<b>Normal / Heroic / Nightmare</b>), <b>Panzer Difficulty</b> (<b>Easy / Medium / Nightmare</b>) and <b>PaP Pricing</b> (<b>Scaling / Fixed</b>) in pregame settings. Defaults are <b>Normal</b> zombie growth, <b>Medium</b> Panzers and <b>Scaling</b> PaP prices, which cap at round 21 (<b>50,000 upgrade / 25,000 repack</b>). See <a href="#progression">Health &amp; progression</a> and <a href="#economy">Points &amp; upgrades</a> for the full tables.')}
      ${note('A different rhythm from round one', 'You start with <b>0 points, 130 HP and sprinting zombies</b>. Round breaks are short, so familiar opening-round habits can leave you surrounded sooner than expected. The early pressure is intentional, and a few short runs can help you find the rhythm. Start with the basics below; the rest of this guide is here when you need it.')}
      <div class="section-heading"><h2>Finding your feet: the first five rounds</h2><span>SPACE, HEADSHOTS, SURVIVABILITY</span></div>
      <ol class="steps"><li><div><strong>Make space, then shoot.</strong><p>Keep an escape route in mind. Use unlimited sprint, wide turns and slides to reposition instead of slowly backing into a corner while firing. Learn bunny-hop chaining as you get comfortable; Exo jumps and air dashes come later, at 2,000 kills.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Make headshots an early goal.</strong><p><b>25 headshots</b> unlock a chance to return a round to the magazine on eligible shots. <b>50</b> unlock bonus income at future round starts. Controlled shots help ammunition and progression. Work toward those milestones at your own pace; reaching them by round five is not required.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Use your punch with an exit plan.</strong><p>One Inch Punch is a strong early melee option. Pick off isolated enemies to save ammunition, then move. Standing in a crowd and trading hits for extra points can end a run quickly.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Buy breathing room and survivability.</strong><p>Open a useful route before your current space becomes a trap. Work toward Juggernog and consider Quick Revive’s added health recovery. Read their <a href="#perks">perk entries</a>: familiar names often have new benefits. Reload and make purchases during clear moments; round transitions offer little extra downtime.</p></div></li>
      <li><div><strong>Keep the first run simple.</strong><p>Choose <b>Classic</b> to learn the movement, pacing and progression before adding a blessing’s tradeoffs. Classic disables the blessing only; the rest of Inky Zombies stays active. Once the rhythm clicks, explore the <a href="#blessings">other blessings</a> at your own pace.</p></div></li></ol>
      <h2>Learn in the optional host sandbox</h2><p>The host is an admin by default, including in solo. After any opening blessing vote, stand still on the ground, hold <b>Sprint for 0.75 seconds</b>, then release. Once the menu opens, temporary damage protection and enemy-ignore behavior give you room to browse Grant, Match and Debug tools. Try milestones, choose a blessing or enable personal <b>5× EOC fast charge</b> to practice with the elements. See <a href="#controls">Controls &amp; settings</a> for examples.</p><p>The match keeps running. Menu protection ends when it closes, including after 30 seconds without input. <b>Non-admin teammates remain vulnerable</b>; send points during a clear moment or with someone covering you. Other admins get the host’s protection too.</p>
      <h2>What you are building toward</h2><p>Your first goal is room to breathe. Longer runs reward you with <b>2× / 3× / 4× total kill-tier damage</b> at 500 / 1,000 / 1,500 personal kills by default, personal <b>permanent Double Points at 100 headshots</b>, extra perk rolls after 200 headshots and <b>Exo movement at 2,000 kills</b>. Juggernog grows your maximum HP across later rounds. Overcharge brings four elemental attacks when that blessing is active, while supported added Panzer encounters grow to multiple enemies.</p><p>The host can choose <b>Normal</b> damage values or set <b>Kill Reward Progress</b> to <b>Shared</b> before the match. Its default is <b>Individual</b>; both modes use the same 500 / 1,000 / 1,500 targets. See <a href="#progression">Health &amp; progression</a> for how they work.</p>
      <div class="topic-grid">${topic('progression', 'Progression rewards', 'See headshot and kill milestones, Juggernog growth and Exo unlocks.')}${topic('boss-rush', 'Boss Rush', 'See every encounter roster, cumulative unlock and clear reward.')}${topic('blessings', 'Spiritual Blessings', 'Choose a shared modifier or explore the Usury payoff.')}${topic('overcharge', 'Elemental Overcharge', 'Learn charging, arming, each element and boss interactions.')}${topic('perks', 'Reworked and custom perks', 'Find added benefits and custom-perk acquisition routes and Boss Rush perk rewards.')}</div>
      <h2>Load up and set your preferences</h2><p>Subscribe to Inky Zombies, let Steam finish downloading, then select <b>Inky Zombies</b> from BO3’s <b>Mods</b> menu before entering Zombies. You still need your chosen map installed. Everyone in co-op should load the same mod version.</p><p>In the Zombies lobby, the host can use <b>INKY SETTINGS</b> to choose Zombie Difficulty, PaP Pricing, the blessing, zombie-cap mode and kill-reward settings, or leave the blessing to an in-game vote. Random includes Usury, Overcharge and Loaded Dice; it never selects Classic or Boss Rush. Choose Boss Rush explicitly for its encounter schedule and earned blessing chain. Optional starting-pistol choices appear near spawn after the ballot.</p><p><b>Ascension Monkey Rounds defaults to Disabled.</b> Choose <b>Vanilla</b> in INKY SETTINGS before launching if you want its native monkey rounds or plan to complete the main Easter egg. See <a href="#maps">Maps &amp; compatibility</a> for the rewards and quest impact.</p><p>For Boss Rush, select it under <b>Spiritual Blessing</b>; on a custom map, also enable <b>Boss Rush on Custom Maps</b> before launching. See the <a href="#boss-rush">Boss Rush guide</a>. This is separate from ordinary added Panzer encounters.</p><p>Want ordinary Panzer encounters on a custom map? The host must set <b>Custom Map Panzers → On</b> in INKY SETTINGS <b>before launching</b>. It defaults to <b>Off</b> and adds encounters only where the map is compatible. See <a href="#panzers">Panzer encounters</a> for details.</p><p><b>Auto EC is on by default</b>: it spends 100,000 points once you have 500 headshots and 100,000 spendable points. <b>Auto Overcharge is off by default</b>; enable it for your player to arm full charges automatically for the next qualifying hit. Personal choices apply on the next match connection and last for this BO3 session. Combat hotkeys and automation keep menu use optional.</p>
      <h2>About this reference</h2><p>This guide describes the <b>Inky Zombies 1.3 default rules</b>. Host settings and map-specific systems can affect a run. Official maps receive dedicated adjustments; custom-map support depends on the systems that map uses. Check <a href="#maps">Maps &amp; compatibility</a> for differences. If something seems broken, report the map, round, blessing and what happened; a clip helps.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'controls', nav: 'Controls & settings', title: 'Set up your run.', intro: 'This guide uses the Special Action names shown in BO3’s Keybinds menu. The usual keyboard layout is 1 = nuke, 2 = random perk, 3 = trip mine, 4 = arm Overcharge. If you rebound an action, use your chosen key.', keywords: 'keybind special action 3 special action 4 key 4 action slot 3 action slot 4 trip mine menu shift controller d pad settings auto EC automatic arm admin debug fast charge',
      body: `${note('Menu safety depends on your role', '<b>Solo / host:</b> you are an admin by default, so opening the menu grants temporary invulnerability and enemy-ignore protection for browsing and experimenting. <b>Non-admin co-op player:</b> opening the menu grants no protection. Other admins receive the host’s protection too. The match keeps running, and menu protection ends on closing or the 30-second idle auto-close.')}
      <h2>In-match actions</h2>${table(['Action', 'Input', 'What it does'], [
        ['Open player menu', 'Stand still, grounded; hold Sprint 0.75 s, then release', 'Usually Shift / L3. Available after the blessing ballot.'],
        ['Buy a nuke', 'Hold Special Action 1 for 0.5 s', 'Usually 1 / D-pad Up. Costs 30,000; also available in the player menu.'],
        ['Buy random perk', 'Hold Special Action 2 for 0.5 s', 'Usually 2 / D-pad Down. Buys once per hold.'],
        ['Equip trip mine', 'Press Special Action 3', 'Usually 3 / D-pad Right. Uses the equipment assigned to this action.'],
        ['Arm / cancel Overcharge', 'Tap Special Action 4', 'Usually 4 / D-pad Left. Requires a full stored bar; hit an eligible enemy afterward to cast.'],
        ['Contribute to a door', 'Melee a supported unopened door while touching it', 'Pay half once. You or a teammate then use Interact to pay the remainder and open it.'],
        ['Share your box pull', 'Melee near your offered weapon in co-op', 'Lets another player interact with it to take it.'],
        ['Share your Wunderfizz bottle', 'Melee beside the machine when your rolled bottle is ready', 'In co-op, an eligible teammate can Interact to take that bottle at no extra cost.'],
        ['Cycle golden-bucket water', 'Zetsubou: press Melee + Sprint together with a golden bucket', 'Changes water color and refills it; 3.75 seconds between changes.'],
        ['Tombstone sacrifice', 'While downed with your Tombstone marker active, release Use / Interact, then hold it for 1.5 s', 'Co-op only, with a living, upright teammate. Release to cancel; unavailable while being revived. Ends this life and uses normal respawn timing. Never available in solo.']
      ])}
      ${note('Why some notes say “Action Slot 3” for key 4', 'BO3’s visible Special Action labels and its internal Action Slot names swap the last two numbers: <b>Special Action 3 = internal Action Slot 4</b> (usually key 3, trip mine); <b>Special Action 4 = internal Action Slot 3</b> (usually key 4, Overcharge). Actions 1 and 2 keep the same numbers. Follow the Keybinds menu labels above when choosing or rebinding your keys.')}
      <p>Special Action 4 arms Overcharge with upgraded weapons and on Origins and custom maps. A native action on the same binding, such as a staff’s alternate shot, can happen on the same press. Special Actions 1 and 2 can also conflict with map equipment.</p>
      <h2>Inside the menu</h2>${table(['Input', 'Menu action'], [['Attack / Fire', 'Next row'], ['Aim down sights', 'Previous row'], ['Use / Interact', 'Select or increase'], ['Special Action 2 (usually 2)', 'Decrease'], ['Sprint or Special Action 3 (usually 3)', 'Next page'], ['Crouch', 'Close']])}
      <p>The player pages include AFK, teammate tracking, the blessing readout, buying a nuke, alternate Overcharge arming, and sending points. A manual EC purchase is available if you disable Auto EC. To share points, choose a recipient and amount before selecting Send. If you are not an admin, do this in a clear moment between rounds or with a teammate covering you. AFK is a separate protected state with three uses of up to eight minutes; it prevents you from dealing damage.</p>
      <h2>INKY SETTINGS in the Zombies lobby</h2>${table(['Setting', 'Who controls it', 'Default / behavior'], [['Zombie cap', 'Host', 'Gradual to 32; optional 32 from round one.'], ['Zombie Difficulty', 'Host', 'Normal (default): 14% health growth from round 10. Heroic: 17%. Nightmare: 20%. All cap at 800,000 HP.'], ['PaP Pricing', 'Host', 'Scaling (default): 5k / 20k / 30k / 50k at rounds 1–4 / 5–10 / 11–20 / 21+. Repack costs half. Stops rising at round 21. Fixed: 20k / 10k throughout.'], ['Spiritual Blessing', 'Host / in-game vote', 'Vote in game. The final selection applies to the lobby.'], ['Ascension Monkey Rounds', 'Host', 'Disabled. Ordinary zombie rounds replace monkey rounds. Vanilla restores native monkey rounds; choose it for the main Easter egg.'], ['Custom Map Panzers', 'Host', 'Off. Enable before launching for added Panzers on compatible custom maps. Official maps are unchanged.'], ['Boss Rush on Custom Maps', 'Host', 'Off. Enable before launching, then select Boss Rush as the blessing. Independent of Custom Map Panzers; compatible round and enemy systems are required.'], ['Panzer Difficulty', 'Host', 'Medium (default), Easy or Nightmare. Sets regular Panzer health progression; co-op scaling, the 400,000 HP cap, spawn timing and encounter counts remain the same.'], ['Kill Reward Progress', 'Host', 'Individual (default): your kills. Shared: team kills. Both use 500 / 1,000 / 1,500 at any player count. Exo and headshots stay personal.'], ['Kill Reward Damage', 'Host', 'Retuned: total 2× / 3× / 4×. Normal: total 1.5× / 2.5× / 3.5×. Independent of shared progress.'], ['Auto EC (you)', 'Each player', 'On. Automatically spends 100,000 points once you have 500 headshots and 100,000 spendable points. Turn it off here to buy manually.'], ['Automatically arm Elemental Overcharge', 'Each player', 'Off. Arms your full bar; a qualifying hit still releases it.']])}
      <p><b>Set automation before the match:</b> Auto EC buys when you qualify. Auto Overcharge arms a full bar when available; your next qualifying hit releases it. The kill that fills the bar cannot also release it. Neither action needs the menu during combat.</p>
      <p>Auto EC and Auto Overcharge are personal preferences, saved for the current BO3 session and applied on the next match connection. Turning on automatic arming does not select or grant the Overcharge blessing.</p><p>Host match settings apply to everyone and stay fixed for the match. Set them before launching. Custom session presets save all host settings, including Zombie Difficulty, PaP Pricing, Custom Map Panzers, Boss Rush on Custom Maps and Panzer Difficulty. <b>Built-in presets reset Zombie Difficulty to Normal, PaP Pricing to Scaling, both custom-map toggles to Off and Panzer Difficulty to Medium.</b> Match presets leave your personal Auto EC and Auto Overcharge choices unchanged.</p>
      <h2>Saving a custom session preset</h2><ol><li>As host, open <b>INKY SETTINGS</b> before launching. Select <b>Load Inky Defaults</b> at any time to restore the standard rules.</li><li>Adjust your preferred match settings, such as <b>Zombie Difficulty</b>, <b>PaP Pricing</b>, <b>Panzer Difficulty</b>, <b>Kill Reward Progress</b> or custom-map encounter toggles.</li><li>Select <b>Save Custom for This Session</b> to store your combination, then return to the lobby and start a fresh match. Use <b>Load Session Custom</b> to reapply it in later lobbies during the same BO3 session.</li></ol>
      <h2>An optional sandbox for solo players and hosts</h2><p>Protected menu browsing gives you time to learn the systems. Admins have extra <b>Grant, Match and Debug</b> pages: use Grant’s <b>step/value</b> controls to set your kill count to <b>1,500</b> and try the final personal damage tier with Individual progress selected, choose a blessing to explore, or enable personal <b>EOC fast charge</b> for five-times charging while you practice with Overcharge. These optional sandbox tools let you explore the mechanics; normal progression remains available through play.</p><p>The menu’s protection lasts while it is open. Close it to resume fighting and try your changes; a separate admin god-mode toggle is available when you want continued protection. In co-op, changing a shared match rule or blessing affects the lobby. Lowering counters does not remove unlocks you have already earned during that match.</p>
      <h2>At the blessing ballot</h2><p>Tap <b>Crouch</b> to browse choices and hold <b>Use</b> for roughly 0.35 seconds to vote. You can change your vote before the ballot closes. It lasts up to 20 seconds, closes early when everyone votes, and resolves tied leaders randomly.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'progression', nav: 'Health & progression', title: 'Grow stronger as you survive.', intro: 'Damage milestones use your kills by default, with optional shared team progress. Headshot rewards, spending and Exo stay personal. Earned rewards persist through ordinary downs and respawns within the match; they are not account progression.', keywords: 'health HP maxHP Juggernog jugg damage multiplier kills headshots double points exo tiers kill reward progress individual shared team retuned normal tuning sniper shotgun weapon buff WA2000 Model 1887 Olympia',
      body: `<h2>Health and Juggernog</h2><p>You begin with <b>130 maximum HP</b>. Fresh Juggernog adds 70, bringing an unmodified player to <b>200 HP</b>. Each later round you begin alive and upright while holding it adds another <b>3 maximum HP</b>.</p><p>Buying Juggernog on round 20 gives 200 HP; starting round 21 with it gives 203. It does not award health for earlier rounds. Losing the perk, going down, dying or respawning resets this earned stack. Other blessings can change the ceiling: Nitrogen Cooled removes the flat bonus, while Elemental Overcharge introduces repairable scars.</p>
      ${note('Current health and maximum health are different', 'A lower health bar from taking damage is not the same as missing maximum HP. Overcharge charge acceleration uses its lost health ceiling. Repairing that ceiling does not itself heal the damage you took.')}
      <h2>Kill damage milestones</h2><p>The host selects <b>Kill Reward Damage</b> in pregame INKY SETTINGS. <b>Retuned is the default</b>; choose Normal for a lower multiplier curve. Both use the same milestone targets.</p>${table(['Kills toward the tier', 'Retuned (default)', 'Normal'], [['0–499', '×1 total', '×1 total'], ['500', '×2 total', '×1.5 total'], ['1,000', '×3 total', '×2.5 total'], ['1,500', '×4 total', '×3.5 total']])}<p>Each row is the <b>total kill-tier multiplier</b>, replacing the earlier tier. The final Retuned tier is 4×, not 2 + 3 + 4 added together. Eligible perk and EC bonuses still combine with ordinary damage; <b>Elemental Overcharge uses its own percentage-health damage</b>.</p>
      <h2>Weapon damage tuning</h2>${table(['Supported weapon or family', 'Inky damage adjustment'], [['Sniper rifles', '3× bullet damage'], ['Shotguns, including Olympia', '2× bullet damage'], ['Sheiva, MX Garand and M14', '3× bullet damage'], ['M8A7, XR-2, Pharo, RK5 and M16', '2× bullet damage'], ['Ray Gun', '2× damage'], ['Ray Gun Mark II', 'One-third damage']])}<p><b>Base and Pack-a-Punched versions qualify.</b> Availability still depends on the map. The named semi-auto, burst and Ray Gun adjustments apply to their supported weapon families; custom versions can differ.</p><p>Custom sniper rifles and shotguns such as the <b>WA2000</b> and <b>Model 1887</b> receive their class bonus when the map identifies the weapon class correctly and their attacks use bullet damage. Each eligible hit receives its weapon adjustment once; matching both a supported name and its class does not apply it twice. Special weapon rules, map overrides and enemy protections can still affect the result.</p>
      <h2>Individual or Shared progress</h2><p><b>Kill Reward Progress defaults to Individual.</b> Choose <b>Shared</b> before the match to pool everyone’s kills toward just the three damage milestones. Everyone then receives each damage tier together, using either Normal or Retuned values. Your scoreboard, headshot rewards, point earnings, EC purchase and wallet bonus remain personal.</p>${table(['Progress mode', 'First damage tier', 'Second tier', 'Third tier'], [['Individual (default)', '500 of your kills', '1,000 of your kills', '1,500 of your kills'], ['Shared', '500 team kills', '1,000 team kills', '1,500 team kills']])}<p><b>Both modes use the same targets in solo, duo, trio and four-player games.</b> Shared totals combine the lobby’s kills; each player does not need to reach the target separately. Departing players’ contributions remain, and late joiners inherit the team’s earned damage tier.</p><p>While shared damage tiers are still ahead, the reward HUD says <b>Team kills</b> and shows the next shared target and selected damage value. After all three damage tiers, it returns to your personal progress toward <b>Exo Suit at 2,000 kills</b>. Exo unlocks from your own kills regardless of when the team finishes its damage tiers.</p>
      <h2>Headshot milestones</h2>${table(['Your headshots', 'Reward'], [['25', '1-in-10 chance to return a round to the magazine on an eligible shot.'], ['50', 'Round-start score bonus: total kills ×4, rounded down to ten-point steps and capped at 10,000 before multipliers.'], ['66', 'Six-Shooter: improves ammo conservation to 1-in-6, replacing the earlier chance.'], ['100', 'Permanent Double Points for your own qualifying score awards.'], ['200', 'A subsequent eligible perk acquisition also grants a random missing perk.'], ['500', 'Unlocks Extravagant Consumption. Auto EC is on by default and buys it for 100,000 points once you can afford it.']])}
      <p>The 100-headshot reward also doubles eligible damage credits, round-start bonuses and incoming transfers; it is personal, not a whole-team powerup. The 200-headshot reward can grant custom perks, does not chain endlessly, and is not triggered by Tombstone restoring your inventory.</p>
      <h2>Round pressure</h2><p>Ordinary zombies sprint from round one and the extra wait between rounds is removed. The target alive cap stays at 24 through round four, grows by one per round, and reaches 32 at round 12. The host can choose a target of 32 from the start. These are ceilings; map paths and spawners still control how many actually arrive.</p>
      <p>The host selects <b>Zombie Difficulty</b> before launching. <b>Normal</b> is the standard curve; Heroic and Nightmare increase health scaling from round 10 onward.</p>${table(['Difficulty', 'Growth from R10', 'R20 HP', 'R25 HP', 'R30 HP', 'First 800k round'], [['Normal (default)', '14%', '10,087', '19,419', '37,387', '54'], ['Heroic', '17%', '13,431', '29,444', '64,551', '47'], ['Nightmare', '20%', '17,743', '44,146', '109,848', '41']])}<p>All profiles share the same rounds 1–9 and <b>800,000 HP cap</b>. The curve includes base increases at rounds 10 and 20 and rounds down each growth step. Round 10 HP is 1,995 / 2,047 / 2,100 respectively. Health-based perks and Overcharge retain their percentage formulas. Special enemies use their own health rules; Panzer Difficulty is separate. Native hellhound rounds are suppressed, so do not depend on their usual end-of-round Max Ammo.</p>
      <h2>Rejoining the fight</h2><p>Spawn loadouts improve with the current round: starter pistol at rounds 1–5, SMG at 6–9, assault rifle at 10–14, and an upgraded assault rifle from 15 where the map has suitable weapons. Spawn/revive catch-up grants include Quick Revive from round five and Juggernog from round ten. These are not automatic gifts to every surviving player at those round numbers.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'economy', nav: 'Points & upgrades', title: 'Turn precision into buying power.', intro: 'The early wallet is tight. Choose how you earn: headshots build lasting income rewards, while melee and Pickpocket can improve immediate payouts.', keywords: 'EC extravagant consumption PAP pack a punch cost price money score earnings hit damage kill headshot melee pickpocket ethereal razor half points double points stack 100 headshots buy perk door box servant arnie ammo',
      body: `<h2>What you earn at the start</h2><p>These are the default <b>ordinary-zombie awards before score multipliers</b>. Kill values already include the base kill payment and finishing bonus; do not add another base payment. Earlier nonlethal hits earn separate damage credit; the killing strike pays the kill reward. Special enemies and map-specific scoring can differ.</p>${table(['Scoring event', 'Base points / credit'], [['Ordinary nonlethal bullet hit, hip-fire or aimed', '5 credit'], ['Other / limb kill, without a finishing bonus', '20'], ['Torso kill', '30'], ['Neck kill', '40'], ['Head / helmet kill', '50'], ['Ordinary direct melee kill', '60'], ['Burn kill', '30']])}
      <p>Hit credit is paid in <b>ten-point chunks</b>, with the remainder carried forward. Two ordinary nonlethal hits pay 10; a single hit may not visibly move the counter. For example, starting with no saved credit, two nonlethal hits followed by a headshot kill earn <b>10 + 50 = 60 points</b>. These are scoring events, so do not assume every pellet or secondary damage effect awards a separate hit payment.</p>
      <h2>How Double Points stacks</h2><p>The <b>100-headshot reward</b> doubles your qualifying income for the match. A temporary <b>Double Points pickup</b> separately doubles ordinary hit and kill awards. Together they give <b>4× the starting award</b>: a 50-point headshot kill becomes 100 with either bonus alone, or 200 with both. Earlier hit credit is additional. When the pickup expires, your personal 2× remains.</p><p>The starting rates above are Inky’s lower base payouts. There is no universal “award full points, then subtract half” step; reaching 100 headshots doubles these Inky values. Your personal bonus also applies to eligible round-start rewards and incoming transfers, which the temporary pickup does not double.</p>
      <h2>Pickpocket and Ethereal Razor</h2>${table(['Award per enemy', 'Base', 'Pickup only', '100 HS only', 'Both'], [['Ordinary melee kill', '60', '120', '120', '240'], ['Ordinary melee + Pickpocket', '100', '200', '200', '400'], ['Razor area-slash kill', '100', '100', '200', '200'], ['Razor area slash + Pickpocket', '140', '140', '280', '280']])}
      <p>The Razor premium belongs to kills made by its <b>separate area slash</b>; direct punch kills use ordinary melee scoring. Its nonlethal slash pays 10 per affected enemy, or 20 with the 100-headshot reward. <b>Temporary Double Points pickups do not multiply Razor’s separate slash awards</b>; the personal 100-headshot reward does. This makes melee useful for immediate income while headshots build your longer-term economy.</p>
      <h2>Key prices</h2>${table(['Purchase', 'Default cost'], [['Pack-a-Punch upgrade / repack', 'Scaling by round; see the phase table below'], ['Bonfire Sale upgrade / repack', 'One-fifth of the current phase prices'], ['Random missing perk', '2,000 + 2,000 per held perk, capped at 30,000'], ['Buyable nuke', '30,000 points'], ['Extravagant Consumption', '100,000 points, after 500 headshots']])}
      <h2>Pack-a-Punch price phases</h2><p><b>PaP Pricing defaults to Scaling</b>. Prices rise only at rounds 5, 11 and 21, then <b>stop increasing</b>. The repack / AAT base price is always half of an upgrade.</p>${table(['Round', 'First upgrade', 'Repack / AAT', 'Bonfire upgrade / repack'], [['1–4', '5,000', '2,500', '1,000 / 500'], ['5–10', '20,000', '10,000', '4,000 / 2,000'], ['11–20', '30,000', '15,000', '6,000 / 3,000'], ['21 onward', '50,000', '25,000', '10,000 / 5,000']])}<p>Choose <b>Fixed</b> in INKY SETTINGS for flat <b>20,000 / 10,000</b> prices at every round, or <b>4,000 / 2,000</b> during Bonfire Sale. These are base prices before other native discounts or exemptions. An early upgrade rewards efficient routing; later upgrades compete with perk purchases and EC wallet damage. Maps without PaP keep their normal weapon progression.</p>
      <h2>Extravagant Consumption</h2><p><b>EC is a personal purchase, not a perk bottle or a blessing.</b> Auto EC is <b>on by default</b>: once you have <b>500 headshots and 100,000 spendable points</b>, it automatically buys EC and deducts <b>100,000 points</b>. It waits while you carry active Usury debt. You can turn Auto EC off in <b>INKY SETTINGS</b> in the Zombies lobby and buy manually from the player menu instead.</p><p>After purchase, every complete <b>10,000 points currently in your wallet</b> adds +100% base damage to the bonus pool used by your kill rewards. Spending score lowers this live bonus; the 100,000 spent on EC does not count toward it.</p>
      ${note('Example: spend first, then calculate', 'With the final kill-damage tier unlocked, active EC and 20,000 points remaining, default Retuned tuning gives <b>4 + 2 = 6×</b> before other eligible effects. Normal tuning gives <b>3.5 + 2 = 5.5×</b>. This works with either personal or shared damage milestones; EC still uses only your own wallet. Buying EC at exactly 100,000 leaves no immediate wallet bonus until you earn more points.')}
      <h2>Co-op purchases</h2><p><b>Share door costs:</b> melee a supported unopened door while touching it to pay half its price. This contribution can happen only once per door. You or a teammate then use <b>Interact</b> to pay the remainder and open it. You can also buy the door outright with Interact. Map-specific doors may use a different system.</p><p><b>Send points:</b> select the player-menu recipient, amount and Send action. Transfers use ten-point steps. Non-admin teammates remain vulnerable while sending, so choose a clear moment between rounds or have someone cover you. A recipient’s permanent Double Points reward can increase what they receive.</p><p><b>Share box pulls:</b> the buyer can melee near their offered weapon to let a teammate take it. Supported box locations are revealed together; the teddy does not relocate the box, and Fire Sale is removed.</p>
      <p><b>Roll your own copy:</b> weapons already unlocked in the map’s box remain available while teammates own base or upgraded copies. Your own duplicate restrictions and quest unlocks still apply; see <a href="#maps">Mystery Box in co-op</a>.</p><p><b>Share Wunderfizz:</b> in co-op, melee beside the machine while your rolled bottle is ready. An eligible teammate can then <b>Interact</b> with the shared offer. The buyer pays for the roll; the recipient pays <b>nothing extra</b>, and only one player receives the bottle. The recipient must be alive and playing, eligible for another perk under the map’s purchase rules, and neither own nor have the offered perk paused. Finish drinking or reviving and put away equipment or a placeable mine before taking it. A shared offer still expires normally; sharing one bottle does not share later rolls.</p>
      <h2>Ammo and equipment</h2><p>Max Ammo refills reserves and supported weapon magazines, so ordinary guns no longer require a pre-pickup reload. New rounds do not automatically replenish grenades. Ammo Regen can supply ammo and equipment over time; mines are excluded from its held-magazine refill.</p><p>Supported weapon magazines use a <b>1.5× size multiplier</b> to help with the larger hordes, including when Head Drama is selected directly or activated through Usury payoff. This is a magazine-size setting, not a blanket reserve-ammo increase, and weapon or map overrides can differ.</p>
      <h2>How the damage bonuses combine</h2><p>Ordinary supported enemy damage combines kill-tier and EC bonuses additively into one multiplier. The eligible sniper or shotgun weapon buff also applies. Mule Kick contributes its own 25% boost; eligible Deadshot hits gain their headshot bonus. Armor, hit locations and weapon rules still matter.</p><p><b>Explosive Bullets starts from 30% of the bullet's damage before Inky's bonuses.</b> Distance falloff happens before the blast receives its own applicable kill-tier/EC, fire and Mule Kick bonuses once. Danger Close then adds 3% of the victim's maximum HP without amplifying that addition.</p><p><b>Elemental Overcharge does not inherit EC, kill-tier, Mule Kick, Deadshot or Danger Close amplification.</b> Its casts use each target’s maximum-health profile. Ethereal Razor’s special health-based strike is also separate from the normal multiplier stack.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'blessings', nav: 'Spiritual Blessings', title: 'Choose your run’s direction.', intro: 'The lobby chooses a Spiritual Blessing together. Boss Rush earns cumulative benefits through its encounter clears. Headshot rewards, spending and elemental charge stay personal even when everyone shares the same blessing.', keywords: 'pickpocket locksmith trickster gersh arnie head drama nitrogen cooled usury debt classic random vote boss rush loaded dice mystery box wonder weapon',
      body: `<h2>The choices</h2>${table(['Blessing', 'What changes', 'Tradeoff / condition'], [
        ['Pickpocket', 'Ordinary direct melee kills pay 100 points; Ethereal Razor’s separate area-slash kills pay 140, before applicable score bonuses.', 'No additional explicit drawback.'],
        ['Locksmith', 'Receive roughly half the price back after buying a supported door.', 'You must afford the upfront price. Half-pay contributions themselves do not refund.'],
        ['Trickster', 'Adds a Trickster powerup drop. Collect it to deploy a Little Arnie on Shadows of Evil, active for six seconds after settling, or a six-second Gersh on Ascension and Moon. Other supported maps use a monkey-bomb decoy.', 'Deploys where collected; its kills and points belong to the collector. Never adds or replaces your tactical equipment. Native enemy immunities still apply.'],
        ['Head Drama', 'During Insta-Kill, bullet hits become head hits; redirected body shots gain a 2× boost.', 'Replaces Insta-Kill’s guaranteed one-shot kills with bullet hits that count as headshots. This helps you earn powerful headshot rewards sooner, but gives up the powerup’s usual safety net when you’re surrounded.'],
        ['Nitrogen Cooled', 'While alive and upright with Juggernog, nearby supported zombies walk inside a 110-unit aura, checked every 0.2 s.', 'Removes Juggernog’s flat +70 HP; earned round health remains.'],
        ['Usury', 'Pay off everyone’s debt to activate the blessing benefits together, without the Juggernog health penalty, Overcharge scars or Loaded Dice box tax. Boss Rush is excluded.', 'Debt, interest, reload tax and temporary ammo financing before payoff.'],
        ['Elemental Overcharge', 'Bank and release one of four elemental attacks.', 'Ordinary zombie swipes reduce maximum HP; kills repair it.'],
        ['Loaded Dice', 'Eligible Wonder Weapons and special box tacticals receive 3× roll weight on rounds 1–9, 4× on rounds 10–19 and 5× from round 20.', 'Ordinary Mystery Box spins cost 2,000 points. Fire Sale keeps its discounted price.'],
        ['Boss Rush', 'A boss encounter every third round, with clear rewards and cumulative blessing unlocks. See the <a href="#boss-rush">Boss Rush guide</a>.', 'Choose it explicitly. Custom maps also need Boss Rush on Custom Maps enabled before launch.'],
        ['Random', 'Picks one blessing for the lobby; Usury, Elemental Overcharge and Loaded Dice are eligible.', 'Classic and Boss Rush are excluded.'],
        ['Classic', 'No Spiritual Blessing.', 'The rest of Inky Zombies stays enabled.']
      ])}<p>If nobody votes, the fallback picks from Pickpocket, Locksmith, Trickster, Head Drama and Nitrogen Cooled. It does not force debt, Overcharge scars or the Loaded Dice box tax onto an abstaining lobby.</p>
      <h2>Loaded Dice: pay more for better rolls</h2><p>The bonus applies to eligible Wonder Weapons and special tacticals such as Monkey Bombs, Gershes and Little Arnies. Ray Gun, Mark II, KT-4 and an unlocked Apothicon Servant benefit when their map permits them in the box. <b>The multiplier changes roll weight, not a fixed percentage chance or a guaranteed drop.</b> Your inventory, quest unlocks and the map’s eligibility checks still apply. Supported custom-map registrations can receive the bonus too; a custom weapon must expose compatible weapon metadata and use the standard box selection path.</p><p>Selecting Loaded Dice directly makes ordinary spins cost <b>2,000 points</b>; Fire Sale retains its discounted price. Earning Loaded Dice through <b>Usury payoff</b> or the <b>Boss Rush wave-9 clear</b> grants the weight bonus with <b>no box tax</b>, preserving the map’s ordinary price.</p>
      <h2>Usury: earn your way out</h2>${table(['Players', 'Starting debt per player', 'Combined starting debt'], [['Solo', '3,000–5,000', '3,000–5,000'], ['Duo', '2,500–4,000', '5,000–8,000'], ['Trio', '2,000–3,000', '6,000–9,000'], ['Quad', '1,250–2,000', '5,000–8,000']])}<ol class="steps"><li><div><strong>Start in debt.</strong><p>Each player rolls a separate debt using the connected lobby size when their debt is first assigned, rounded down to ten-point steps. Existing debts do not change when someone joins or leaves. Earnings reduce that personal debt before becoming spendable score. A red debt readout can appear while your ordinary wallet remains at zero.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Keep ahead of the interest.</strong><p>About 1.5% is added every nine seconds, rounded up to ten-point increments. Interest pauses while downed and stops at 12,500 debt; other charges can still push debt above that value.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Watch your reloads.</strong><p>Reloads cost ten points until the lobby payoff. While you personally owe and an eligible ordinary firearm has no ammo, Usury can finance a replacement magazine, adding one debt per round fired. Wonder weapons and gadgets are excluded.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Clear the whole lobby.</strong><p>The payoff begins once every connected player is debt-free. A late join before payoff participates; a join after payoff does not restart debt.</p></div></li></ol>
      ${note('The payoff', 'Pickpocket, Locksmith, Trickster, Head Drama, Nitrogen Cooled, Elemental Overcharge and Loaded Dice activate together. Interest and reload tax stop. Juggernog keeps its <b>+70 HP bonus</b>, Overcharge works without reducing your maximum HP, and Loaded Dice improves the box rolls without its 2,000-point price.')}
      <p>Head Drama turns bullet hits into headshots during Insta-Kill, helping you earn headshot rewards but giving up guaranteed one-shot kills. A redirected body-shot kill earns headshot credit only after the enemy actually dies from that hit; boss armor, closed Margwa mouths and other protected phases still apply. Natural headshots keep their normal credit.</p><p><b>Usury payoff does not start Boss Rush.</b> Random and an unanswered ballot never select Boss Rush either; the mode requires an explicit choice.</p>
      <p>Overcharge fills an empty bar with <b>20 qualifying direct zombie kills</b>, at five charge each. See <a href="#overcharge">Elemental Overcharge</a> for which kills count, how to release a charge and when you can charge again.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'boss-rush', nav: 'Boss Rush', title: 'Every third round.<br>Earn your next advantage.', intro: 'Boss Rush turns rounds 3, 6, 9 and every third round after them into finite boss encounters. Clear the entire roster to earn ammunition, points and a growing set of blessing benefits.', keywords: 'boss rush 1.3 update margwa panzer meatball sprinter roster schedule wave rewards custom maps origins kino concurrency max ammo head drama trickster loaded dice',
      body: `<h2>Choose Boss Rush before the run</h2><p>In the Zombies lobby, select <b>INKY SETTINGS → Spiritual Blessing → Boss Rush</b>. You can also choose it explicitly in the opening blessing vote. For a custom map, the host must first enable <b>Boss Rush on Custom Maps</b> before launching. That setting defaults to <b>Off</b> and is independent of <b>Custom Map Panzers</b>: enabling ordinary added Panzers does not enable Boss Rush, and Boss Rush does not require that other toggle.</p><p>Random, an unanswered ballot and Usury payoff never start Boss Rush. Because every third round is replaced by a dedicated boss encounter, Boss Rush is built for survival play and can interrupt main Easter egg steps that depend on standard round events.</p>
      <h2>The encounter rhythm</h2><p>You receive a warning on the round before each boss encounter. Rounds <b>1–2</b> are the opening recovery rounds, followed by the first encounter on <b>round 3</b>. Each successful clear leads to <b>two ordinary recovery rounds</b>. Round 36 is the twelfth and largest programmed roster. Rounds <b>39, 42, 45 and onward</b> repeat that finale roster while Panzer health continues along its encounter curve up to the 400,000 HP cap.</p><p><b>Panzers, Margwas, ordinary zombies and Shadows meatballs spawn independently.</b> A waiting meatball does not require the zombie queue to wait. Each heavy type and the meatball stream use roughly one-second spacing between arrivals; ordinary zombies have no added stagger. Native spawn animations, available space and engine slots still affect the actual arrival time.</p><p>The whole listed <b>Panzer and Margwa roster can be alive together</b>. Ordinary zombies have a separate <b>32-alive ceiling</b> and at most <b>64 total per encounter</b>. Shadows meatballs allow <b>four alive in solo / six in co-op</b>. These are shared encounter totals and limits, not quantities per player; they do not guarantee every type can fill its limit simultaneously when engine slots are occupied.</p>
      <h2>Shadows of Evil roster</h2><p><b>P = Panzer, M = Margwa, B = meatball, Z = ordinary sprinting zombie.</b> Each row gives the complete encounter roster. All 2–4-player lobbies use the co-op counts.</p>${table(['Wave / round', 'Solo roster', 'Co-op roster: 2–4 players'], [
        ['1 / 3', '1M + 6B', '1M + 10B'],
        ['2 / 6', '2M + 8B + 18Z', '2M + 14B + 32Z'],
        ['3 / 9', '1P + 10B', '1P + 16B'],
        ['4 / 12', '1P + 1M + 10B + 18Z', '1P + 2M + 16B + 32Z'],
        ['5 / 15', '1P + 2M + 12B + 18Z', '2P + 2M + 18B + 32Z'],
        ['6 / 18', '2P + 2M + 12B + 18Z', '2P + 3M + 20B + 48Z'],
        ['7 / 21', '2P + 3M + 14B + 24Z', '3P + 3M + 22B + 48Z'],
        ['8 / 24', '3P + 3M + 14B + 24Z', '3P + 4M + 24B + 48Z'],
        ['9 / 27', '3P + 4M + 16B + 32Z', '4P + 4M + 24B + 48Z'],
        ['10 / 30', '4P + 4M + 16B + 32Z', '4P + 5M + 26B + 64Z'],
        ['11 / 33', '4P + 5M + 18B + 32Z', '5P + 5M + 28B + 64Z'],
        ['12 / 36', '5P + 5M + 18B + 32Z', '6P + 5M + 28B + 64Z']
      ])}
      <h2>Other official and compatible custom maps</h2><p>These maps use <b>ordinary sprinters instead of meatballs</b>. The Panzer and Margwa schedule is the same as Shadows; the zombie quotas below form a separate roster.</p>${table(['Wave / round', 'Solo roster', 'Co-op roster: 2–4 players'], [
        ['1 / 3', '1M + 8Z', '1M + 14Z'],
        ['2 / 6', '2M + 24Z', '2M + 40Z'],
        ['3 / 9', '1P + 12Z', '1P + 20Z'],
        ['4 / 12', '1P + 1M + 24Z', '1P + 2M + 44Z'],
        ['5 / 15', '1P + 2M + 32Z', '2P + 2M + 44Z'],
        ['6 / 18', '2P + 2M + 32Z', '2P + 3M + 56Z'],
        ['7 / 21', '2P + 3M + 40Z', '3P + 3M + 56Z'],
        ['8 / 24', '3P + 3M + 40Z', '3P + 4M + 64Z'],
        ['9 / 27', '3P + 4M + 48Z', '4P + 4M + 64Z'],
        ['10 / 30', '4P + 4M + 48Z', '4P + 5M + 64Z'],
        ['11 / 33', '4P + 5M + 56Z', '5P + 5M + 64Z'],
        ['12 / 36', '5P + 5M + 56Z', '6P + 5M + 64Z']
      ])}<p>The first Panzer encounter, on round 9, uses Panzer health tier 1. Later boss waves advance that tier once per encounter, not once per Panzer. <b>Panzer Difficulty</b> and co-op health scaling apply normally; see <a href="#panzers">Panzer encounters</a> for the health curve and 400,000 HP cap.</p>
      <h2>Clear rewards and cumulative blessings</h2><p>Boss Rush begins with <b>Locksmith</b>. Each encounter clear gives <b>Max Ammo</b> and a fixed cash reward to each eligible participant: <b>1,000 on the first clear, 2,000 on the second, then 5,000 on every later clear</b>. Player count and Double Points do not multiply this cash.</p>${table(['Clear / round', 'Additional reward'], [
        ['Wave 1 / round 3', 'Pickpocket'],
        ['Wave 2 / round 6', 'Random missing eligible perk'],
        ['Wave 3 / round 9', 'Head Drama'],
        ['Wave 4 / round 12', 'Random missing eligible perk'],
        ['Wave 5 / round 15', 'Trickster'],
        ['Wave 6 / round 18', 'Random missing eligible perk'],
        ['Wave 7 / round 21', 'Nitrogen Cooled, retaining Juggernog’s +70 HP'],
        ['Wave 8 / round 24', 'Random missing eligible perk'],
        ['Wave 9 / round 27', 'Loaded Dice without the 2,000-point box tax'],
        ['Wave 10 / round 30', 'Random missing eligible perk'],
        ['Wave 11 / round 33', 'Elemental Overcharge without maximum-HP scars'],
        ['Wave 12 / round 36', 'Random missing eligible perk'],
        ['Every later boss encounter', 'Random missing eligible perk']
      ])}<p>Each newly unlocked blessing is announced to everyone. Blessing benefits accumulate for the lobby and survive individual deaths. Later joiners receive the unlocked blessing benefits, but do not receive earlier personal cash or perk awards. Selecting another blessing resets these accumulated benefits.</p><p>Personal rewards belong to players participating when the encounter begins who remain connected. Downed players are included; players already dead or spectating at the start are not. An eligible player who goes down or dies during the fight keeps the earned award while connected; perk rolls and ammunition wait until that player is ready. Perk rewards choose from the map’s registered perks you do not already own, retain the normal <b>200-headshot extra-perk benefit</b>, and follow normal perk loss rules. If no eligible perk remains, there is no substitute cash payout. Boss Rush does not start a Bonfire Sale.</p>
      <h2>Support drops during a boss encounter</h2><p>During boss encounters, ordinary zombie kills can drop <b>Insta-Kills after Head Drama unlocks</b> and <b>Trickster pickups after Trickster unlocks</b>. These use normal powerup drop chances and limits: there are <b>no kill thresholds or guaranteed pickups</b>, and neither type is limited to one per encounter. Other ordinary random powerups remain blocked; they are not replaced with extra Insta-Kills or Trickster pickups. Bosses, meatballs and enemies removed without kill credit do not produce these support drops. A map must support the pickup and permit the drop at that location. Recovery rounds retain their normal drop rules, and boss-clear rewards are unchanged.</p><p>Head Drama still changes Insta-Kill: eligible bullets become head hits, with the redirected body-hit bonus, instead of guaranteed one-shot kills. It respects armor and closed Margwa mouths. Only a confirmed kill earns redirected headshot credit; a blocked or nonlethal hit does not advance headshot rewards.</p>
      <h2>Map compatibility</h2><p>Boss Rush is supported on official survival maps except <b>The Giant and Verrückt, which are currently unsupported</b>. Shadows of Evil uses its native meatballs alongside the bosses, while other supported official maps use sprinting zombie rosters. Heavy bosses require valid navigation paths and spawn space, so tight map layouts or active map events can affect where bosses enter. <b>Verrückt also does not support ordinary added Panzer encounters</b>.</p><p>On custom maps, Boss Rush is opt-in via <b>Boss Rush on Custom Maps</b>. Compatibility depends on the map using standard round progression, valid navigation mesh and spawn space for heavy bosses, registered perks and standard powerup drops. Custom maps with bespoke round controllers or custom boss scripts may conflict with encounter scheduling. If an encounter cannot continue and is aborted by the failsafe, no clear reward is granted.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'overcharge', nav: 'Elemental Overcharge', title: 'Charge. Arm. Hit. Unleash.', intro: 'Four distinct attacks, one personal meter. Use the moment and the terrain: a wide blast, a branching chain, a freezing field or repeated boss-focused explosions.', keywords: 'EOC elemental charge scars meter lightning wind ice fire panzer margwa boss damage range ticks cooldown stun',
      body: `<div class="meters" aria-label="Four elements"><span style="--accent:var(--lightning)">LIGHTNING</span><span style="--accent:var(--wind)">WIND</span><span style="--accent:var(--ice)">ICE</span><span style="--accent:var(--fire)">FIRE</span></div>
      <h2>Using a charge</h2><ol class="steps"><li><div><strong>Fill the bar to 100.</strong><p>Direct bullet or melee kills of ordinary zombies add charge, including upgraded guns and eligible direct Insta-Kill kills. Secondary AAT effects, grenades, traps, teammates’ kills and elemental casts do not refill it. Special-enemy and boss kills do not add charge.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Bank it, then arm it.</strong><p>Tap Special Action 4, usually keyboard 4 / D-pad Left (internal Action Slot 3). Tap again to cancel without spending it. You can also arm from the player menu or enable personal automatic arming in the lobby settings.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Land a qualifying direct hit.</strong><p>Your next accepted bullet or melee hit releases the cast at the target. Arming alone does nothing; the kill that fills the bar cannot also release it. Wind requires a non-boss trigger.</p></div></li><li><div><strong>Let it finish and cycle.</strong><p>Charging pauses for at least 12 seconds from release. A longer Lightning chain must also finish. The next element is chosen equally from the other three; it cannot repeat immediately, but this is not a guaranteed four-element rotation.</p></div></li></ol>
      <p>A fresh active life starts on a random element. Going down, dying, respawning or changing the blessing resets the normal charge state. Opening the menu or entering a new round disarms an armed charge without spending it; automatic arming resumes when available. Manually cancelling keeps that bank stored even with automatic arming enabled, until you choose to rearm it. Kills during cooldown do not bank the next meter.</p>
      <h2>The health tradeoff</h2><p>This penalty applies when selecting Overcharge directly. <b>Paid-off Usury and Boss Rush’s wave-11 Overcharge unlock have no maximum-HP scars.</b></p><p>Taking a hit from an ordinary zombie removes <b>5 maximum HP</b> in addition to its normal damage. A qualifying ordinary zombie kill repairs <b>2 maximum HP</b>. Repair does not heal current HP. Overcharge’s own zombie kills can repair scars but cannot add charge.</p><p>The scar floor is <b>60% of your normal maximum HP, rounded up to ten</b>: 80 from a 130-HP ceiling, or 120 from a 200-HP ceiling. Missing maximum HP speeds charging, sampled before the kill repairs it:</p><p><code>charge per direct kill = 5 + floor(10 × lost max HP / available scar pool)</code></p><p>That is 5–15 charge per eligible kill. At full maximum HP, it takes 20 kills. A fully scarred next kill gives 15, but later kills may give less as scars heal. Ordinary low current health is not a charge bonus. Paid-off Usury and Boss Rush’s Overcharge unlock stay at five charge per qualifying kill.</p>
      <h2>Current element profiles</h2><p>All ranges below are <b>radii in game units</b>, measured from the cast or chain emitter. Percentages show the base maximum-health damage dealt per target before boss armor, invulnerability phases and map-specific resistances. Solid geometry can block hits.</p>
      <div class="element-list">
      ${element('Lightning', 'var(--lightning)', 'BRANCHING HORDE CLEAR', [['777', 'link radius'], ['≈1 s', 'relay windup'], ['110%', 'ordinary max HP / zap'], ['12%', 'boss max HP / zap']], `<p>The initial source scans immediately. Each admitted enemy becomes its own relay: after roughly one second, it checks nearby targets and resolves its zap. Killing an admitted relay does not erase its scheduled propagation.</p><p>Link chance is <b>100% through 333 units</b>, falls linearly to <b>66% at 444</b>, then to <b>33% at 777</b>. A target missed by one emitter can be considered by another. The whole chain stays within <b>2,000 units of the first proc</b> and admits up to <b>32 targets</b>, each hit once.</p><p>During the windup, affected zombies are slowed to 5% animation speed while humanoid bosses move at 65%. Flying and rolling enemies (such as parasites, meatballs and spiders) still chain and take damage, but keep their normal movement speed. A sparse line, blocked sightline or failed outer-range roll can end a branch.</p>`)}
      ${element('Wind', 'var(--wind)', 'IMMEDIATE WIDE BLAST', [['1,250', 'blast radius'], ['1', 'instant pulse'], ['200%', 'ordinary max HP'], ['25%', 'boss max HP']], `<p>A single wide, immediate blast around the proc position, affecting up to <b>32 targets</b>. Full 3D distance and visibility apply.</p><p><b>Boss hits cannot release Wind.</b> The charge stays armed and unspent. Trigger on an ordinary or eligible small enemy near the boss to deal the indirect boss damage.</p>`)}
      ${element('Ice', 'var(--ice)', 'PERSISTENT AREA CONTROL', [['240', 'field radius'], ['6 s', 'field duration'], ['20%', 'ordinary max HP / pulse'], ['2%', 'boss max HP / pulse']], `<p><b>Twelve pulses total, one every 0.5 seconds</b>, starting at activation. The field stays at the original location and can catch new arrivals after the trigger enemy dies. Up to 32 targets per pulse; 128-unit vertical limit and visibility checks.</p><p>Affected ground enemies are slowed to <b>10% animation speed</b> while inside the field, with each pulse refreshing the slow until they leave. Flying and rolling enemies such as parasites, meatballs and spiders take full pulse damage but are not slowed.</p><p>A target held in the field for all twelve pulses takes up to <b>240% ordinary / 24% boss</b> total maximum-health damage. Lure the horde through the field; its coverage is much smaller than Wind.</p>`)}
      ${element('Fire', 'var(--fire)', 'REPEATED BOSS BURST', [['500', 'blast radius'], ['0.9 s', 'between blasts'], ['250%', 'ordinary max HP / blast'], ['60%', 'boss max HP / blast']], `<p><b>Three blasts</b> at approximately 0, 0.9 and 1.8 seconds, all at the original proc position. Up to 16 targets per blast, prioritizing bosses. Visibility and a 128-unit vertical limit apply.</p><p>A target caught in all three blasts takes up to <b>750% ordinary / 180% boss</b> total maximum-health damage. The blast does not follow a moving boss, and invulnerable phases still matter.</p>`)}
      </div>
      <h2>Panzer and Margwa trigger rules</h2>${table(['Element', 'Direct Panzer trigger', 'Direct Margwa trigger', 'Indirect boss damage'], [['Lightning', 'Damaging bullet or melee hit', 'Damaging hit to an open head', 'Panzer: yes. Margwa: visible vulnerable head required.'], ['Wind', 'No', 'No', 'Panzer: yes. Margwa: visible vulnerable head required.'], ['Ice', 'Damaging bullet or melee hit', 'Damaging hit to an open head', 'Panzer: yes. Margwa: visible vulnerable head required.'], ['Fire', 'Damaging bullet or melee hit', 'Body hits allowed while the enemy is damageable', 'Distributes damage across Margwa heads, including closed mouths outside immunity phases.']])}
      <p>Margwa percentages use the combined three-head maximum-health pool. Spawn/teleport immunity and head transitions remain protected. Panzer armor and native map rules still apply; these percentages do not guarantee that every boss dies in a fixed number of casts.</p>
      ${note('Casts and charging have different target rules', 'Small special enemies such as Nova Crawlers, parasites, rolling meatballs and spiders can be cast targets, but they do not charge the bar or repair scars. Thrashers, Manglers, Valkyries and the Moon astronaut use boss damage profiles, including Wind’s direct-trigger restriction.')}
      <p>Elemental damage does <b>not</b> inherit kill rewards, EC, Mule Kick or secondary perk procs. These are Inky’s elemental effects, not a grant of the native upgraded Origins staff weapons.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'perks', nav: 'Perk reference', title: 'Build around your playstyle.', intro: 'Many familiar BO3 perks are retuned, expanded or reworked, alongside Inky’s custom perks. The full roster contains eight native perk identities and ten Inky perks, including a replacement Widow’s Wine. Availability depends on the map; custom maps may add their own perks.', keywords: 'reworked retuned expanded native custom perk bottle random purchase 200 headshots juggernog quick revive speed cola double tap stamin up deadshot mule kick electric cherry phd flopper tombstone ethereal razor ammo regen dying wish widows wine burn heart danger close magnet explosive bullets',
      body: `<h2>Familiar perks with new benefits</h2><p>Read the entries below for each perk’s Inky behavior: <b>Juggernog gains maximum HP each new round</b>, <b>Quick Revive adds faster health recovery</b>, and <b>Mule Kick boosts damage</b> while everyone already starts with three weapon slots. The Native label identifies a perk’s origin; many of those perks have changed effects. Electric Cherry retains its map’s native behavior.</p>
      <h2>How to get custom perks</h2><p><b>These routes can award Inky custom perks as well as available native perks</b>, choosing a random perk you do not already own:</p><ul><li><b>Random perk bottle drop:</b> awards a random missing supported perk to each eligible active player.</li><li><b>Random perk purchase:</b> hold <b>Special Action 2 (usually key 2 / D-pad Down) for 0.5 seconds</b>. Costs <b>2,000 + 2,000 per held perk</b>, capped at 30,000.</li><li><b>200-headshot reward:</b> unlocks an extra random perk roll when you subsequently acquire an eligible perk. Custom perks are included in that bonus roll; reaching 200 headshots alone does not immediately grant a perk.</li><li><b>Boss Rush clear rewards:</b> designated encounters grant a personal random missing eligible perk. The 200-headshot benefit applies to these awards too. See the <a href="#boss-rush">reward schedule</a>.</li></ul><p>The extra-perk reward does not chain into further rewards, and Tombstone restoring your perks does not trigger it. The normal four-perk restriction is removed.</p>
      <p>Supported vending machines still provide their named perks. The ordinary Wunderfizz rotation contains supported base-game perks, including Inky Widow’s Wine in place of native Widow; it does not contain the entire custom roster. Use the routes above to access the broader perk pool.</p>
      <div class="perk-filter" aria-label="Filter perk list"><button class="filter-button" data-perk-filter="all" aria-pressed="true">All 18</button><button class="filter-button" data-perk-filter="stock" aria-pressed="false">Native 8</button><button class="filter-button" data-perk-filter="inky" aria-pressed="false">Inky 10</button></div>
      ${(window.INKY_PERKS || []).map(p => `<details class="perk" data-perk-type="${p.type}"><summary><span><strong>${p.name}</strong><small>${p.summary}</small></span><span class="perk-type">${p.type === 'stock' ? 'NATIVE' : 'INKY'}</span></summary><div class="perk-copy">${p.detail}</div></details>`).join('')}
      ${note('Health-based damage is not always boss true damage', 'Danger Close additions and ordinary perk effects still pass through the enemy’s normal protections. Burn Heart’s lingering pools damage ordinary zombies, not Panzers or Margwas. Elemental Overcharge has its own separate boss handling.')}`
    },
    {
      id: 'panzers', nav: 'Panzer encounters', title: 'Hear the landing. Make room.', intro: 'Origins-style Panzers bring the grappling claw and flamethrower to more official maps and, with the host setting enabled, compatible custom maps. Later encounters add bodies as well as health.', keywords: 'panzer soldat mech claw HP scaling boss encounter origins DE der eisendrache kino non native custom map panzers town reimagined host off on difficulty easy medium nightmare',
      body: `${note('Playing Boss Rush?', 'The schedule below describes ordinary Panzer encounters. Boss Rush has its own every-third-round roster and permits the full listed heavy group together. Its Panzers use the same health profiles. <b>Boss Rush is currently unsupported on The Giant and Verrückt.</b> The Giant remains in the ordinary added-Panzer schedule below; Verrückt is excluded from added Panzer encounters. See <a href="#boss-rush">Boss Rush</a> for its schedule and separate custom-map toggle.')}<h2>Added encounters</h2><p>Panzers are added to <b>Shadows of Evil, The Giant, Zetsubou No Shima, Gorod Krovi, Nacht der Untoten, Shi No Numa, Kino der Toten, Ascension, Shangri-La and Moon</b>. Moon’s Area 51 is excluded, and <b>Verrückt</b> is excluded due to its tight spawn layout.</p><h2>Custom maps: enable before launching</h2><p>The host can set <b>INKY SETTINGS → Custom Map Panzers → On</b> before starting a fresh match. It defaults to <b>Off</b>. Compatible custom maps use the added-encounter schedule, counts and health below; no separate per-map setup is needed. This choice stays fixed for the match and does not change official-map encounters.</p><p>Added Panzer encounters work on most custom maps. Maps with their own Panzer systems or conflicting behavior may prevent added encounters, and unusual layouts can leave no suitable spawn location. If a map has trouble, leave this setting Off for your next match. Loading a built-in preset resets it to Off; your saved custom session preset remembers the choice.</p><h2>Added encounter schedule</h2>${table(['Encounter', 'Nominal round', 'Panzer count'], [['1', '8', '1'], ['2', '14', '1'], ['3', '20', '2'], ['4', '26', '2'], ['5 onward', '32, then every 6 rounds', '3']])}<p>These counts apply in solo and co-op. Multiple Panzers arrive roughly three seconds apart. Surviving Panzers, special rounds and suitable spawn placement can postpone the next encounter; a partially spawned group does not spill into a new round.</p>
      <h2>Choose your Panzer difficulty</h2><p>The host selects <b>INKY SETTINGS → Panzer Difficulty</b> before launching. <b>Medium is the default.</b> Your custom preset remembers the choice; built-in presets restore Medium. The selected profile stays fixed for the match.</p>${table(['Difficulty', 'First encounter: solo HP', 'Increase for encounter 2', 'Increase for each later encounter'], [['Easy', '4,000', '+15,000', '+20,000'], ['Medium (default)', '8,000', '+15,000', '+25,000'], ['Nightmare', '16,000', '+20,000', '+40,000']])}
      <h2>Health per Panzer</h2>${table(['Encounter tier', 'Easy: solo', 'Medium: solo', 'Nightmare: solo'], [['1', '4,000', '8,000', '16,000'], ['2', '19,000', '23,000', '36,000'], ['3', '39,000', '48,000', '76,000'], ['4', '59,000', '73,000', '116,000'], ['5', '79,000', '98,000', '156,000']])}
      <p>Multiply by <b>1 / 1.5 / 2.25 / 3</b> for one through four players, with a final cap of <b>400,000 HP per Panzer</b>. Multiple Panzers in the same encounter share the same tier rather than advancing it individually. Difficulty scales health progression and proportional armor durability, while encounter timing, Panzer counts and attack behavior remain the same. This setting does not enable custom-map encounters by itself; ordinary added encounters still need <b>Custom Map Panzers → On</b>. Custom Boss Rush instead requires its own <b>Boss Rush on Custom Maps</b> setting.</p>
      <div class="calc" id="panzer-calculator" data-web-only><div><span class="eyebrow">ENCOUNTER PLANNER</span><h3>Health per Panzer</h3><p>Regular encounter health before armor and hit-location rules.</p></div><div class="calc-controls"><label>Difficulty<select name="difficulty"><option value="easy">Easy</option><option value="medium" selected>Medium</option><option value="nightmare">Nightmare</option></select></label><label>Encounter tier<input name="tier" type="number" min="1" max="100" step="1" value="3"></label><label>Players<select name="players"><option value="1">Solo</option><option value="2">2 players</option><option value="3">3 players</option><option value="4">4 players</option></select></label></div><output aria-live="polite">48,000 HP</output></div>
      <h2>Maps with native Panzers</h2>${table(['Map', 'Native encounter pattern'], [['Origins', 'First nominally round 8 after its zone gate, then every 4 rounds. Solo: one. Co-op: one for encounters 1–2, two for 3–5, three from 6.'], ['Der Eisendrache', 'First round 12, then native 5–6-round gaps. Solo: one. Co-op: one for encounters 1–2, two for 3–4, three from 5.'], ['Revelations', 'Retains its native boss rotation. Its second boss event is a Panzer, nominally round 18–21; later boss events can select Panzer or Margwa.']])}
      <p>Regular supported native Panzers use the same health curve. Quest and boss-fight variants can keep their own tuning. Native armor, weapon reductions and vulnerable hit locations remain relevant.</p>
      <h2>Explosive Bullets, frags and Danger Close</h2><p>Regular Panzers on <b>Origins, Der Eisendrache and Revelations</b> and our added Panzers use their full encounter HP for Danger Close, including difficulty and co-op scaling. Taking damage does not reduce that maximum-health value.</p><p>These Panzers normally take <b>half damage from Explosive Bullets and standard frags</b>, including Danger Close's bonus. With Danger Close, Explosive Bullets therefore adds <b>1.5% of maximum body HP</b> and a frag adds <b>10%</b>, plus half the blast's other damage. These percentages are not caps on total damage. Without Danger Close, there is no percentage-health addition.</p><p><b>Blowing off the faceplate does not change explosion resistance.</b> Direct bullets still follow each Panzer's armor and weak-point rules. Active Insta-Kill removes the half-damage reduction: the Danger Close portions become <b>3% / 20%</b>, plus the blast's other damage. Other weapons and quest or boss-fight variants use their native damage rules.</p>
      <h2>Fighting the claw</h2><p>When a Panzer grabs you with its claw, it holds position while reeling you in, then attacks with its flamethrower at close range. Leave room to break away. Lightning, Ice and Fire can release from a damaging Panzer hit; Wind needs a nearby non-boss trigger.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'movement', nav: 'Movement & melee', title: 'Keep your momentum.', intro: 'Unlimited sprint, extended slides and a short bhop timing window reward an active route through the horde.', keywords: 'bhop bunny hop movement chain window 150ms slide sprint exo jump dash ground slam OIP one inch punch melee knife',
      body: `<h2>Bhop timing</h2><p>Jump promptly after landing to continue the chain. The current window is <b>150 milliseconds from landing to the next takeoff</b>. Successful chaining adds 0.40 to the assist multiplier, capped at 1.55: <b>1.00 → 1.40 → 1.55</b>.</p><p>The assist uses a 220-unit-per-second reference, targeting 308 then 341 units per second. Steering and momentum conditions still apply; this is not a constant movement bonus. Stamin-Up adds its own 1.05 movement multiplier, while unlimited sprint is already baseline.</p><p>Slides receive an initial assist and a longer easing phase while the native slide continues. Terrain and entry speed affect the result.</p>
      <h2>One Inch Punch</h2><p>On non-Origins maps, One Inch Punch replaces the starting knife. Its base hit is <b>2,250 damage within 64 units</b>, with a half-strength <b>1,125-damage reach out to 100 units</b> inside its forward cone. Supported ordinary enemy hits can benefit from normal damage bonuses.</p><p>Special contact handling covers Nova Crawlers, Shadows parasites and Zetsubou spiders so melee does not fall back to a one-damage contact on those native enemy families. Custom enemy systems may differ.</p><p>Origins keeps its native punch progression: complete the soul boxes to upgrade it, with element selection tied to a carried upgraded staff. That punch progression is separate from the Elemental Overcharge blessing.</p>
      <h2>Exo Suit at 2,000 personal kills</h2><p>Choosing Shared progress does not share or lower this requirement. Your own kills unlock Exo in either damage-tuning mode.</p><p><b>After takeoff, release both Jump and Sprint</b> before using an Exo action.</p>${table(['Action', 'Input while airborne', 'Energy cost'], [['Extra jump', 'Tap Jump', '20'], ['Air dash', 'Tap Sprint while moving horizontally', '20'], ['Ground slam', 'After an Exo jump, Crouch with Double Tap owned', '30']])}<p>The air dash boosts your current direction of travel. Each action can be used once per airtime. The pool holds 100 energy and refills on the ground at ten every 0.1 seconds. The ground slam requires Double Tap.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'maps', nav: 'Maps & compatibility', title: 'Old routes. New conveniences.', intro: 'Inky changes the survival rules across official maps and supports standard custom-map systems. Map-owned quests and enemy behavior still shape what is available.', keywords: 'Origins shadows evil SOE Ascension monkey rounds space monkeys perk stealing main Easter egg custom maps town reimagined compatibility servant apothicon lil arnies dig shovel helmet staff weapon roster Der Eisendrache DE Void crawlers dragon plunger clock wisps Ragnarok Zetsubou ZNS golden bucket water mystery box copies PAP reservation',
      body: `<h2>Across supported maps</h2><ul><li>Three primary weapon slots from the start; Mule Kick adds damage instead of a fourth slot.</li><li>Supported mystery-box locations are revealed together, without teddy relocation.</li><li>Share box pulls and contribute half the price of supported doors in co-op.</li><li>Window boards are removed; Carpenter and Fire Sale are removed from the normal drops.</li><li>Impact frags, improved Max Ammo refills and supported starting mines change equipment use.</li><li>Alive-zombie caps, health progression and round pacing follow the Inky defaults unless host or map behavior changes them.</li></ul>
      <h2>Weapon rosters</h2><p>Each official and custom map uses its own weapon pool. Inky Zombies applies its three-weapon baseline, 1.5× magazine capacities, supported equipment upgrades and weapon-class damage buffs across those map rosters.</p>
      <h2>Origins</h2><ul><li>Start with One Inch Punch, G-Strikes and a shovel.</li><li>Gramophone and disc collection are skipped, with streamlined chamber and portal access.</li><li>Crafted-staff upgrade puzzles and the crypt ritual are skipped; the Crazy Place charger still needs <b>20 souls</b>.</li><li>Crazy Place walls stay up.</li><li>The golden helmet is granted after <b>four total digs</b>, followed by the golden shovel on the <b>fifth</b>.</li><li>Tank cooldown is capped at <b>five seconds</b>.</li><li>An active bhop chain bypasses mud slowdown.</li></ul><p>Special Action 4 (usually key 4 / D-pad Left) still arms Overcharge here, even though a staff can use the same binding for its native alternate action.</p>
      <h2>Der Eisendrache</h2><ul><li><b>Void bow:</b> during the sacrifice step, bring ordinary living zombies into the active ritual circle. They are automatically prepared as crawlers for the skulls. You still need <b>six live sacrifices</b>; killing zombies does not replace them. Automatic preparation stops when the step ends, and the later rune and bow steps remain.</li><li><b>Dragons:</b> eligible kills in a dragon’s feeding area can count while it is chewing. Saved credit applies after its current animation finishes, up to the normal <b>eight feeding credits</b>. Feeding animations keep their normal speed; this does not guarantee completion in exactly eight deaths.</li><li><b>Optional plunger clock:</b> complete the usual fire-bow wall and trap-form Ragnarok placement prerequisites to power the clock. You can then pull the clocktower lever at <b>any displayed time</b> to open the wallrun route. The wallrun and plunger visit/pickup still need to be completed.</li><li><b>Wisp hunts:</b> find and shoot all <b>four glowing objects with an upgraded bow</b>. You have <b>135 seconds per target</b>; a failed attempt restarts after <b>nine seconds</b> without waiting for another round. Wrong hits and round changes still fail the hunt. After success, use the normal teleport window before the next round.</li><li><b>Boss Rush Ragnaroks:</b> defeating a Boss Rush Panzer can drop the native Ragnarok body part, including the first Panzer encounter on <b>round 9</b>. Pick it up normally. If an uncollected pickup times out, a later Panzer defeat can offer it again until it is collected.</li></ul><p>The fire bow’s midair shot keeps the normal launch speed and controls.</p>
      <h2>Zetsubou No Shima</h2><p>Start each life with a <b>golden bucket</b>, including late joins and respawns. A first grant or ordinary-bucket upgrade starts with <b>full blue water</b>; an existing golden bucket keeps its water. While you have it, the golden bucket <b>refills after water use</b>, so your water supply does not run out. With the golden bucket, press <b>Melee + Sprint together</b> to cycle water colors and refill it, with <b>3.75 seconds between changes</b>. Cycling is unavailable while drinking or helping a revive.</p><p>Planting, generators and the golden-bucket quest retain their usual interactions. Losing or planting a bucket during a life, or being revived, does not immediately grant another one; the automatic grant returns on a new life.</p>
      <h2>Mystery Box in co-op</h2><p>Every weapon already admitted to the current map’s box can remain available while teammates own base or upgraded copies. This includes eligible wonder weapons, Ray Gun Mark II, Gersh Devices and Lil’ Arnies <b>where the map offers them</b>. Map unlocks still apply; this does not add missing weapons, craft-only bows or staves to the box.</p><p>You still cannot roll another copy of a weapon family you already carry. For weapons that normally have a lobby limit, your own copy in Pack-a-Punch also prevents another roll while it is in the machine. A known teammate’s PAP copy does not prevent your roll. The Ray Gun/Mk II restriction and Revelations’ one Servant-or-Thundergun family per player remain.</p>
      <h2>Shadows of Evil</h2><p>Players receive the fumigator automatically when the native pod system is present. After the map unlocks the Apothicon Servant, multiple players can obtain it through eligible mystery-box rolls. Lil’ Arnies are also available to multiple players. This does not skip the Servant’s map unlock or let one player carry a second Servant-family weapon.</p>
      <h2>Ascension</h2><p>The host's pregame <b>Ascension Monkey Rounds</b> setting defaults to <b>Disabled</b>: ordinary zombie rounds replace the perk-stealing space monkey rounds. Their guaranteed end-of-round Max Ammo and free-perk bonus are absent too.</p><p>Select <b>Vanilla</b> before launching to restore native monkey rounds, perk stealing and their usual rewards. <b>Choose Vanilla for the main Easter egg:</b> its synchronized switch step requires a monkey round. This setting affects only Ascension; other maps and special enemies keep their existing behavior, including the separate suppression of hellhound rounds.</p>
      <h2>Gersh Devices on Ascension and Moon</h2><p>Both player-thrown Gershes and Trickster Gershes pull eligible enemies within a <b>1,500-unit radius</b>. Pulling becomes faster inside <b>1,000 units</b>; the native lethal collapse remains inside <b>128 units</b>. These are distances from the device, not diameters, and native enemy immunities still apply.</p><p>Trickster deploys its Gersh where the powerup is collected, active for <b>six seconds</b>, with kills and points credited to the collector. It does not grant equipment, teleport players or advance Gersh quest steps. Player-thrown Gershes keep their normal duration, teleport and quest behavior. Ammo-regeneration rules are unchanged.</p>
      <h2>Special enemies</h2><p>Elemental casts include support for known native families such as dogs, Nova Crawlers, hostile Keepers, Furies, parasites, rolling meatballs, spiders, Valkyries, Thrashers, Manglers, the astronaut, Panzers and Margwas. Boss profiles and invulnerable phases still apply. Damage support does not mean every enemy is slowed or counts toward charging.</p>
      <h2>Boss Rush on official and custom maps</h2><p><b>The Giant and Verrückt are currently unsupported for Boss Rush.</b> Verrückt also lacks ordinary added Panzer encounters. On supported maps, choose <b>Boss Rush</b> explicitly under Spiritual Blessing. Shadows of Evil pairs the boss schedule with its native meatballs, while other official and compatible custom maps use sprinting zombie rosters alongside Panzers and Margwas. On a custom map, enable <b>Boss Rush on Custom Maps</b> before launch; <b>Custom Map Panzers</b> is a separate setting for ordinary added encounters. Custom maps require standard round progression, valid heavy-boss navigation and spawn space, and standard perk and powerup support. See the <a href="#boss-rush">Boss Rush guide</a> for full rosters and compatibility details.</p>
      ${note('Custom-map support', 'Standard kill charging and Special Action 4 activation (usually key 4 / D-pad Left) are designed to work on custom maps. Replaced enemy scripts, bespoke weapons, perk machines and custom quests can need specific support. The host can enable added Panzer encounters with <b>Custom Map Panzers → On</b> before launching. Added encounters work on most custom maps; the setting defaults to <b>Off</b>. Existing Panzer systems, conflicting behavior or unsuitable spawn locations can prevent added encounters. See <a href="#panzers">Panzer encounters</a> for the schedule and compatibility details.')}
      <h2>If something behaves differently</h2><p>Record the map name, round, active blessing, weapon and upgrade state, enemy type, and what you expected versus what happened. For Overcharge, include the element and whether the meter said Charging, Ready, Armed or Cooldown. A short clip showing the meter and the target is more useful than the effect alone.</p><p>After an update, let Steam finish downloading, restart BO3 and load the updated mod. Keep co-op players on the same version, and avoid confusing a separate old local copy with the Workshop install.</p>`
    },
    {
      id: 'faq', nav: 'Quick answers', title: 'The questions that come up mid-run.', intro: 'A compact reminder of the rules that are easiest to mix up.', keywords: 'FAQ help not charging no charge arming slow damage perk confusion',
      body: `<h2>How do I start Boss Rush on a custom map?</h2><p>Before launch, the host must enable <b>Boss Rush on Custom Maps</b> and select <b>Boss Rush</b> under Spiritual Blessing, or select it in the opening vote. The ordinary <b>Custom Map Panzers</b> toggle does not enable Boss Rush. Read the <a href="#boss-rush">encounter and reward schedule</a>.</p><h2>Does Burn Heart block Panzer flamethrowers?</h2><p>No. Burn Heart protects against environmental fire and its own fire pools; it does not reduce Panzer flamethrower damage.</p><h2>Why is my elemental meter not increasing?</h2><p>You need the Overcharge blessing, paid-off Usury or Boss Rush’s wave-11 unlock, and the meter must be in its charging state. Direct ordinary-zombie bullet/melee kills count; boss kills, special-enemy kills, secondary AAT effects, traps and the elemental cast itself do not. Cooldown kills do not count toward the next bar.</p>
      <h2>Does Scaling PaP keep getting more expensive?</h2><p>No. The default upgrade / repack prices are 5,000 / 2,500 on rounds 1–4, 20,000 / 10,000 on rounds 5–10, 30,000 / 15,000 on rounds 11–20 and <b>50,000 / 25,000 from round 21 onward</b>. That last phase is the ceiling. Select <b>PaP Pricing → Fixed</b> before launching for 20,000 / 10,000 throughout.</p>
      <h2>Does Zombie Difficulty change my kill rewards or Panzers?</h2><p>No. It selects ordinary-zombie health growth: <b>Normal 14%, Heroic 17% or Nightmare 20%</b> from round 10, with an 800,000 HP cap. <b>Kill Reward Damage</b> and <b>Panzer Difficulty</b> are separate settings; choosing Normal zombie health does not change the default Retuned kill rewards.</p>
      <h2>Do I need to lose maximum HP to charge?</h2><p>No. At full maximum HP, qualifying kills add five charge. Scars increase that gain, up to 15 for the next fully scarred kill. Usury payoff and Boss Rush’s wave-11 unlock have no scars and use five per kill.</p>
      <h2>Why did shooting a boss not release Wind?</h2><p>Wind cannot trigger directly on bosses. Use an ordinary or eligible small enemy nearby; the blast can still damage the boss. Lightning, Ice and Fire have separate direct-hit and vulnerability rules.</p>
      <h2>Does Ice do twelve ticks every half-second?</h2><p>No: <b>twelve ticks total</b>, one every half-second over roughly six seconds. It remains at the original proc location. Its 240-unit radius is much smaller than Wind’s 1,250.</p>
      <h2>Why can a special enemy move normally through Ice?</h2><p>Ice slows ground enemies by reducing their movement animation speed. Flying and rolling enemies such as parasites, meatballs and spiders still take full elemental damage, but are not affected by the movement slow. Native control effects and protected phases can also affect the result.</p>
      <h2>Will the rotation always visit all four elements?</h2><p>No. After any spent element, the next is chosen from the other three. Immediate repeats are prevented; seeing the same two elements alternate is possible.</p>
      <h2>Does Classic restore vanilla Zombies?</h2><p>No. It removes the Spiritual Blessing only. Perks, progression, movement, economy and other mod changes remain.</p>
      <h2>Does EC boost elemental procs?</h2><p>No. EC, kill rewards and Mule Kick affect eligible ordinary damage, while elemental casts use their own percentage-health profiles. EC also uses your current remaining wallet, not the points spent buying it.</p>
      <h2>Why do I already have three guns without Mule Kick?</h2><p>Three slots are baseline. Mule Kick adds a 25% damage boost on supported enemy damage; it does not add a fourth slot.</p>
      <h2>Where are the custom perk machines?</h2><p>Most Inky perks are acquired through the paid random perk action or Inky’s perk bottle, rather than dedicated machines. The regular Wunderfizz pool is primarily supported base-game perks.</p>
      <h2>Where should I look first?</h2><div class="topic-grid">${topic('controls', 'Need the right button?', 'Menu controls, arming, point purchases and personal preferences.')}${topic('overcharge', 'Need the exact numbers?', 'Ranges, chances, tick rates and boss behavior for all four elements.')}</div>`
    }
  ];
})();
